        <body>
            <div xml:id="d192-a2">

                <pb facs="0363" n="263" sameAs="frus:frus1884/pg263"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 2 in No. 61.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Gilman to Mr. Frelinghuysen.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Bible House, Astor Place</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">New
                                York</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1884-05-22"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">May</hi> 22,
                    1884</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In connection with the suggestions
                    contained in my letter of yesterday, I beg leave to call your attention to an
                    extract from a recent letter from Dr. Bliss, under date of May 5, having special
                    reference to the embarrassments which Turkish officials throw in the way of
                    circulating books which are expressly sanctioned by the Government censor. The
                    cases are parallel to those occurring in Greece, and their importance is such as
                    to justify our reference to the State Department, with the request that Mr.
                    Wallace may be suitably instructed to secure protection for our interests so far
                    as it may be in his power.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>EDWARD W. GILMAN,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Corresponding
                            Secretary.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
